  "result": {
    "research": [
      "Findings — Anthropic's official guidance on system prompt length:\n\n- **No explicit \"keep system prompts under X tokens\" number exists in Anthropic's official docs.** Their guidance is qualitative (\"Goldilocks zone\", \"minimal but sufficient\"), not a hard token ceiling. Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n- **\"Find the smallest possible set of high-signal tokens that maximize the likelihood of [the] desired outcome.\"** This is the operative principle from Anthropic's context engineering post. They explicitly note *minimal ≠ short* — sufficient information up front is required. Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n- **\"Right altitude\" / Goldilocks zone for system prompts:** Anthropic warns against two failure modes — (1) brittle hardcoded if-else logic stuffed into the prompt, and (2) vague high-level guidance that assumes shared context. Optimal = specific enough to guide, flexible enough to leave heuristics. Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n- **Context rot is real and applies to system prompts too:** \"as the number of tokens in the context window increases, the model's ability to accurately recall information from that context decreases.\" Anthropic frames context (incl. system prompt) as a finite \"attention budget.\" Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents (cites https://research.trychroma.com/context-rot)\n\n- **Recommended structure, not length:** Organize with `<background_information>`, `<instructions>`, `## Tool guidance`, `## Output description` sections via XML tags or Markdown headers. \"Start with a minimal prompt … then add clear instructions and examples to improve performance based on failure modes.\" Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n- **Prompt caching minimum cacheable length (the closest thing to a hard threshold):**\n  - **1,024 tokens** for Claude Sonnet 4 / 4.5 / 4.6, Opus 4 / 4.1 / 4.8\n  - **2,048 tokens** for Claude Haiku 3.5 / older Haiku\n  - **4,096 tokens** for Claude Opus 4.5 / 4.6 / 4.7, Haiku 4.5, Mythos Preview\n  - Below threshold, `cache_creation_input_tokens` returns 0 and nothing is cached (silent no-op). Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching\n\n- **Caching hierarchy is `tools → system → messages`** with up to 4 cache breakpoints per request. The system prompt is the canonical \"long stable block\" Anthropic expects you to cache — implying they expect production system prompts to commonly exceed 1,024 tokens, not be aggressively trimmed below. Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching\n\n- **Few-shot examples warning that applies directly to bloated system prompts:** \"teams will often stuff a laundry list of edge cases into a prompt in an attempt to articulate every possible rule … We do not recommend this. Instead … curate a set of diverse, canonical examples.\" Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n- **Token counting endpoint exists specifically to let you measure system prompt size** before sending — Anthropic provides the tool but doesn't publish a target number, reinforcing that the answer is \"measure and tune,\" not \"stay under N.\" Source: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/token-counting\n\n- **Claude Code's own pattern (Anthropic's flagship agent) is hybrid:** `CLAUDE.md` is dropped into context up front, but file contents are fetched just-in-time via glob/grep rather than preloaded — Anthropic's implicit answer to \"how big should the system-level instructions be?\" is \"small enough to leave the attention budget for the actual work; load detail on demand.\" Source: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n\n**Bottom line:** Anthropic publishes no numeric cap on system prompt length. The strongest quantitative anchor is the **1,024-token prompt-caching floor** (Sonnet 4.x / Opus 4.x), which marks where caching starts to pay off — not a ceiling. The qualitative guidance is: minimal-but-sufficient, structured, cache the stable parts, and treat every added token as draw against a finite attention budget.\n\nIf the prompt's longer than this answer, it's probably too long.",
      "I have plenty of evidence. Synthesizing now.\n\n---\n\n# Community findings: oversized system prompts in agent harnesses (2024-2026)\n\n## Key findings (with sources)\n\n- **Claude Code's system prompt is ~19.6K–30K tokens at session start, depending on project config and tools.** Empirically measured via `claude -p --output-format json`: 27,169 tokens in `/tmp`, 30,915 in a real project dir. Tool definitions dominate. ([claudecodecamp.com](https://www.claudecodecamp.com/p/inside-claude-code-s-system-prompt), [yrzhe.top](https://yrzhe.top/project/context-engineering-for-ai-coding-why-your-200k-token-window-is-lying-to-you))\n\n- **Cursor's agent system prompt is ~15K tokens of prose, and a fresh \"hello\" in an empty folder costs 18.5K tokens of overhead.** Cursor's own forum confirms this is \"expected baseline.\" ([weighmyprompt.com](https://weighmyprompt.com/guides/leaked-system-prompts-explained.html), [forum.cursor.com](https://forum.cursor.com/t/initial-token-count-too-high-18-5k-for-a-new-conversation/152935))\n\n- **Cline's stock system prompt is ~11.7K tokens; the MCP + browse sections alone are 4.7K (~40%).** Maintainers acknowledge this as a quality concern and have shipped configurable slimming. ([cline#1310](https://github.com/cline/cline/discussions/1310))\n\n- **Chroma's \"Context Rot\" study (2024)** showed instruction-following degrades non-uniformly as inputs grow — even on simple tasks well within the advertised window. Degradation starts well before nominal limits. ([trychroma.com/research/context-rot](https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot))\n\n- **\"Lost in the middle\" (Liu et al., TACL 2024)** is the canonical result: accuracy drops sharply when load-bearing content sits mid-prompt vs at the head or tail. Replicated across Claude/GPT/Gemini and reaffirmed in 2025 work like LIFBENCH and \"Context Length Alone Hurts LLM Performance Despite Perfect Retrieval.\" ([ACL](https://aclanthology.org/2024.tacl-1.9/), [LIFBENCH](https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.803.pdf), [findings-emnlp 2025](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1264.pdf))\n\n- **Particula's eval (2025) found a sweet-spot ceiling much lower than people assume**: GPT-5 accuracy drops ~12% past **~4K tokens** of prompt; Claude holds until **~5.5K** before degradation kicks in on instruction-following style tasks. ([particula.tech](https://particula.tech/blog/optimal-prompt-length-ai-performance))\n\n- **Anthropic's own April 2025 post-mortem** admitted a single added system-prompt instruction (to reduce verbosity) measurably hurt coding quality across Sonnet 4.6, Opus 4.6 and Opus 4.7, and had to be reverted. Direct evidence that prompt edits at the margin move quality even on production models. ([anthropic.com/engineering/april-23-postmortem](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/april-23-postmortem))\n\n- **A widely-shared critique of Claude Code's prompt** (gist analysis, 2025) calls out exactly the lost-in-the-middle problem on its safety/critical sections, plus heavy negative-instruction bias and redundancy — i.e., the prompt is large enough that its own structure becomes the risk. ([gist](https://gist.github.com/dollspace-gay/e820e684a7ccf883215a0e6a35be5f47))\n\n- **NIAH-style retrieval drops 30–60 points between 200K and 1M tokens** for every frontier model except Gemini 3 Deep Think (2026 benchmark). The capacity number on the model card is not the usable number. ([digitalapplied.com](https://www.digitalapplied.com/blog/long-context-retrieval-needle-in-haystack-2026))\n\n- **Production prompt anatomy is converging**: leaked-prompts directories (jujumilk3, asgeirtj — 134K+ stars combined) show Anthropic-derived tools (Cursor, Claude Code, Windsurf) all land in the 10–30K token range, heavily XML-sectioned, with 30+ negative rules each. That's the de facto industry band. ([weighmyprompt.com](https://weighmyprompt.com/guides/leaked-system-prompts-explained.html), [augmentcode.com](https://www.augmentcode.com/learn/leaked-ai-system-prompts-github))\n\n## Rough size bands for an agent harness system prompt\n\n| Band | Tokens | Examples / notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lean** | < 3K | Aider-style, SDK minimal preset. Below Particula's degradation knee; behavior dominated by user turn. |\n| **Normal** | 3K – 8K | Sweet-spot per particula.tech evals. Room for tool defs + a coherent persona + key rules without lost-in-the-middle risk. |\n| **Large (industry-standard)** | 8K – 20K | Cline (~12K), Cursor (~15K), leaked Claude Code subsets (~19K). Works in production but measurable quality cost. |\n| **Oversized** | 20K – 35K | Full Claude Code (27–31K). Functional only because of aggressive prompt caching + dynamic-boundary splitting; degradation real, mitigated by structure (XML sections, top/tail placement of critical content). |\n| **Pathological** | > 35K | Begins eating user context budget on 200K-window models; lost-in-the-middle dominates; instruction-following measurably worse per Chroma/LIFBENCH. |\n\n**Bottom line for a new harness:** target **under 8K tokens** if you want pure quality; **8–15K** is the realistic band if you need rich tool docs and behavioral guardrails; **anything above 20K needs caching + cache-boundary structure** to be defensible, and you should expect to pay a measurable instruction-following tax that only careful section ordering (load-bearing content at the head and tail) partially recovers.\n\nSources are linked above per bullet. Big chunks of this evidence — Cursor's 18.5K hello, Claude Code's 30K, Chroma's Context Rot, Particula's 4K/5.5K knee, Anthropic's own postmortem — are independent and converge on the same story: oversized system prompts are real, common in shipped tools, and demonstrably costly.\n\nDone. Turns out the call is coming from inside the prompt.",
      "# Cost & Cache Analysis: Large System Prompts (2025-2026)\n\n## Key Findings\n\n- **Anthropic minimum cacheable block**: 1,024 tokens for Sonnet/Opus, 2,048 for Haiku. Cache is opt-in via explicit `cache_control` breakpoints. ([Cadence guide](https://cadence.withremote.ai/blog/prompt-caching-anthropic))\n- **Anthropic discount math**: Cache *writes* cost **1.25× base** input price (25% premium on first request); cache *reads* cost **0.10× base** (90% discount). ([Claude API docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching))\n- **Anthropic TTL**: Default **5 minutes**, refreshed on every hit (effectively indefinite while a session is active). Optional **1-hour TTL** at 2× write cost ($6/MTok for Sonnet). ([Claude API docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching))\n- **Sonnet 4.x pricing**: $3 / $3.75 (5m write) / $6 (1h write) / **$0.30 (cache hit)** / $15 output, per MTok. ([Claude pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing))\n- **OpenAI prompt caching**: **Fully automatic, no code changes**, no premium on writes. Kicks in at **≥1,024 tokens** with 128-token granularity. Discount is **50% off cached input** (recent GPT-4o/4.1/o-series), and up to ~75-80% on newer snapshots per OpenAI's docs. ([OpenAI docs](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching))\n- **OpenAI cache scope & TTL**: Org-level, exact-prefix match, typically 5-10 min idle / up to 1 hour off-peak. Zero data retention eligible. ([OpenAI cookbook](https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/prompt_caching_201))\n- **Latency bonus**: Anthropic reports up to **85% latency reduction**; OpenAI up to **80%** on long prompts (>10k tokens). ([Introl blog](https://introl.com/blog/prompt-caching-infrastructure-llm-cost-latency-reduction-guide-2025))\n- **Critical gotcha**: Cache is **literal byte-prefix match**. Any change to the system prompt (even whitespace) invalidates the cache and you pay the 25% write premium again. Stable, monolithic prefixes win; interpolated variables near the top destroy savings.\n\n## Worked Example: 4k-token system prompt × 100 requests (Claude Sonnet @ $3/MTok input)\n\n| Scenario | Math | Total |\n|---|---|---|\n| **No cache** | 100 × 4,000 × $3/1M | **$1.200** |\n| **With 5m cache (warm session)** | 1 write: 4k × $3.75/1M = $0.0150<br>99 reads: 99 × 4k × $0.30/1M = $0.1188 | **$0.1338** (**~89% savings**) |\n| **With 1h cache (longer gaps)** | 1 write: 4k × $6/1M = $0.0240<br>99 reads: 99 × 4k × $0.30/1M = $0.1188 | **$0.1428** (**~88% savings**) |\n| **Cache cold every 10 req** (e.g. session gaps > TTL) | 10 writes × $0.0150 + 90 reads × $0.0012 | **$0.258** (~78% savings) |\n\n**Per-request cost**: $0.012 uncached → **$0.0012 cached** (a 4k prompt on a cache hit costs about **1/8th of a cent**).\n\n## Verdict: Does caching amortize the \"too big\" problem?\n\n**Yes, decisively — for repeated sessions with stable prefixes.** A 4k-token system prompt is essentially free once cached: $0.0012/req on Sonnet. Even a 40k-token system prompt drops to ~$0.012/req cached — cheaper than an *uncached* 4k prompt.\n\n**Caveats that re-introduce the problem**:\n1. **One-shot or low-frequency** calls (no second hit before TTL expires) → you eat the 25% write premium on top of full price.\n2. **Volatile prefixes** (user-specific data interpolated near the top, timestamps, request IDs) → no cache hit, ever. Order matters: put stable system instructions first, dynamic content last.\n3. **OpenAI**: 50% discount is meaningful but **half as good as Anthropic's 90%**. The \"amortizes away\" claim is much stronger on Anthropic.\n4. **Cache misses are silent failures** — monitor `cache_read_input_tokens` in API responses or you won't notice you're paying full freight.\n\n**Bottom line**: For an agent harness with a fixed multi-thousand-token system prompt across a session, caching converts the system prompt from a per-request line item into a near-free constant. The 4k → 40k prompt size decision is dominated by *quality* and *context window* tradeoffs, not cost, **provided** the prefix stays byte-stable.\n\nCaching: turning expensive prompts into a rounding error since 2024.",
      "I have enough data. Compiling the apples-to-apples comparison now.\n\n---\n\n# Agent harness system prompt sizes — apples-to-apples for pi-lookback (15,672 chars / ≈3,900 tokens)\n\n**Baseline reference:** pi-lookback ≈ **15.7K chars / ~3.9K tokens**. Below, all numbers are from public measurements (WeighMyPrompt's tiktoken/Claude-tokenizer directory) or from leaked source code with reported counts.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **Cursor — ~10,200 tokens (~38,842 chars)**. Tokenized cl100k_base by WeighMyPrompt; uses XML-tagged sections, Claude-3.5/3.7-Sonnet variant. Leaked multiple times since Apr 2025 (Pliny, jujumilk3, asgeirtj).\n  - https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/cursor\n  - https://github.com/jujumilk3/leaked-system-prompts/blob/main/cursor-ide-sonnet_20241224.md\n\n- **Claude Code — ~15,084 tokens base, but balloons to 23.7K+ in real sessions (and 24K+ \"with tools\")**. Piebald-AI tracks every fragment from the minified npm bundle: the *main* harness instruction string is only ~178 tokens, but Claude Code stitches together 110+ fragments (TodoWrite tool desc alone = 2,037 tks; Workflow tool desc = 4,837 tks; Bash git/PR section = 1,665 tks). Anthropic issue #8245 confirms \"System prompt 23.7k tokens\" with `.git` loaded, ~3.1k without.\n  - https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts (15,084-tk figure)\n  - https://github.com/Piebald-AI/claude-code-system-prompts (per-fragment counts, v2.1.168, Jun 6 2026)\n  - https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/8245\n  - https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43909409 (24k tokens with tools, HN consensus)\n\n- **Devin — ~9,100 tokens (~34,711 chars)**. WeighMyPrompt measures Devin's prompt at 9.1K tk — described there as \"7.1% of model's context window before conversation starts\". Full text mirrored at hussainasghar repo and leaked-system-prompts.com (May 16 2025 leak).\n  - https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/devin\n  - https://leaked-system-prompts.com/prompts/devin/devin-ai-20250516\n\n- **Windsurf (Cascade) — ~3,079 tokens**. The lightest of the major IDE agents per WeighMyPrompt; Claude-3.5 Sonnet variant, XML-tagged sections.\n  - https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/windsurf\n\n- **Cline — ~11,747 tokens (with MCP+browse), ~7,000 without**. Maintainer-confirmed in discussion #1310: \"Currently the system prompt is around 11,747 tokens, with the MCP+browse section alone taking up 4,736 tokens (~40%).\" Issue #9651 cites a ~4,555-tk tool schema serialized into *every* request plus 3–14k of system text.\n  - https://github.com/cline/cline/discussions/1310\n  - https://github.com/cline/cline/issues/9651\n  - Source: https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/main/src/core/prompts/system.ts\n\n- **Aider — ~200–250 tokens main + ~300 reminder (≈740–1,170 chars)**. By far the smallest. PR #4 reduced main system prompt from 924→741 chars and reminder from 1,304→1,168 chars; Paul Gauthier acknowledged token-count optimizations are marginal vs. file context (`repo-map`). Architecture is \"prompt is small, file context dominates.\"\n  - https://github.com/Aider-AI/aider/pull/4\n  - https://github.com/Aider-AI/aider/blob/main/aider/prompts.py\n\n- **Codex CLI (OpenAI) — leaked but no published authoritative tok count; measured ~few thousand tokens of harness text**. Full prompt mirrored at asgeirtj/system_prompts_leaks/OpenAI/codex-cli.md. Codex's documented pathology (issue #16664) is not prompt size but unbounded *tool output* ingestion (one tool output measured at 948,372 tokens).\n  - https://github.com/asgeirtj/system_prompts_leaks/blob/main/OpenAI/codex-cli.md\n  - https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16664\n\n- **Continue, Sourcegraph Cody** — no published per-tool token count found in WeighMyPrompt directory or major leak repos. Continue is broadly characterized as \"small system prompt, chat+completions\" in comparison reviews; Cody is closed-source server-side. Treat as data-not-available.\n\n- **Meta-repos worth citing in any writeup**:\n  - `x1xhlol/system-prompts-and-models-of-ai-tools` — **134K stars**, 28+ tools (Cursor, Claude Code, Devin, Windsurf, Augment, v0, Replit, Lovable, Manus, etc.) https://github.com/x1xhlol/system-prompts-and-models-of-ai-tools\n  - `asgeirtj/system_prompts_leaks` — 41K stars, updated within days of new releases https://github.com/asgeirtj/system_prompts_leaks\n  - `jujumilk3/leaked-system-prompts` — older but well-curated archive\n\n## Where pi-lookback (≈3,900 tk) sits on this spectrum\n\n| Harness | Tokens | vs pi-lookback |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aider | ~250 | 15× smaller |\n| Windsurf | ~3,080 | comparable (0.8×) |\n| **pi-lookback** | **~3,900** | — |\n| Devin | ~9,100 | 2.3× larger |\n| Cursor | ~10,200 | 2.6× larger |\n| Cline (full) | ~11,750 | 3.0× larger |\n| Claude Code (base / no-git) | ~15,084 / 3,100 | 3.9× / 0.8× |\n| Claude Code (with .git + tools) | ~23,700–24,000 | 6× larger |\n\n**Apples-to-apples takeaway:** pi-lookback at ~3.9K tokens is on the **lean end** of the agent-harness distribution — well below Cursor/Cline/Devin/Claude-Code-loaded, roughly even with Windsurf, and an order of magnitude above Aider's repo-map-minimalist design. Only Aider is dramatically smaller, and that's because Aider deliberately offloads everything to a per-file repo map rather than embedding tool/behavior rules in the prompt.\n\nIf the comparison is \"did we go overboard?\" — the answer is no. Cursor and Claude Code, the two harnesses pi-lookback most resembles in tool surface, are both 2.5–6× larger.\n\nNumbers in, conclusions out — that's a wrap. (And if Aider's 250-token prompt is feeling smug right now, remind it that it pays the difference in `repo-map` tokens every single turn.)"
    ],
    "synthesis": "## TL;DR\n**No — 15,672 chars / ~3,918 tokens is not too big.** It sits on the lean end of the agent-harness distribution, comfortably above the prompt-caching floor (1,024 tk on Sonnet) and below the empirical degradation knees that researchers have measured for Claude-class models (~5.5K for instruction-following per Particula, with broader \"context rot\" effects scaling from there) [https://particula.tech/blog/optimal-prompt-length-ai-performance] [https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot].\n\n## Size category\n**Normal (lean end).** At ~3.9K tokens we're inside the 3K–8K \"sweet spot\" band where caching pays off but lost-in-the-middle and instruction-dilution effects are minimal. Cursor (~10.2K), Cline (~11.7K), and Claude Code (~15–24K loaded) are all 2.5–6× larger and still ship in production [https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/cursor] [https://github.com/cline/cline/discussions/1310] [https://github.com/Piebald-AI/claude-code-system-prompts].\n\n## Comparison table\n| Harness | Tokens | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aider | ~250 | [https://github.com/Aider-AI/aider/pull/4] |\n| Windsurf (Cascade) | ~3,080 | [https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/windsurf] |\n| **pi-lookback** | **~3,918** | measured from `pi.buildSystemPrompt()` |\n| Devin | ~9,100 | [https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/devin] |\n| Cursor | ~10,200 | [https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts/cursor] |\n| Cline (full, MCP+browse) | ~11,750 | [https://github.com/cline/cline/discussions/1310] |\n| Claude Code (base) | ~15,080 | [https://weighmyprompt.com/system-prompts] |\n| Claude Code (with .git + tools) | ~23,700–24,000 | [https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/8245] |\n\n## Cost reality check\nClaude Sonnet 4.x: $3/MTok input, $3.75 cache write (5m), **$0.30 cache read**, $15 output [https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing].\n\n100-turn coding session, 3,918-tk system prompt:\n\n| Scenario | Math | Total |\n|---|---|---|\n| **No caching** | 100 × 3,918 × $3/1M | **$1.18** |\n| **5-min cache (warm)** | 1 write: 3,918 × $3.75/1M = $0.0147<br>99 reads: 99 × 3,918 × $0.30/1M = $0.1164 | **$0.131** (~89% saved) |\n| **Cold cache every 10 req** | 10 × $0.0147 + 90 × $0.00118 | **$0.253** |\n\nPer-request cost on a cache hit: **~$0.0012** — about 1/8th of a cent. The system prompt is essentially a rounding error provided the prefix stays byte-stable [https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching].\n\n## When this size becomes a problem\n- **Lost-in-the-middle on load-bearing rules** — applies in principle [https://aclanthology.org/2024.tacl-1.9/], but at 3.9K tokens with critical rules (HARD RULE, Implementation Notes, tool-usage rubric) placed near the top *and* the behavioral_directive injected at the tail, we're already using the standard mitigation. **Low risk for pi.**\n- **Cache invalidation from volatile prefixes** — biggest live risk: AGENTS.md is read on every session, and the embedded \"Current date\" line near the bottom is byte-volatile day-to-day. The cache survives intra-day but breaks at midnight. **Real but cheap — one write/day per session.**\n- **Instruction dilution** — Anthropic's own April 2025 postmortem showed a single added line measurably hurt coding quality across Sonnet/Opus [https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/april-23-postmortem]. The skills listing (~25 skills, ~3K chars of it) is the densest pile of advisory text and the most likely culprit if quality regresses.\n- **Attention budget on long sessions** — at 3.9K, this is a ~2% tax on a 200K window. Negligible until conversation history starts crowding 100K+.\n- **Context rot scaling** — Chroma showed degradation starts well before nominal limits, but the slope at 4K is shallow; this only matters in combination with the above, not on its own [https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot].\n\n## Action items\nNothing urgent. If you want to tune anyway, in priority order:\n\n1. **Make sure the system prompt is being cache-marked.** (a) verify pi sends `cache_control: ephemeral` on the system block; (b) saving: ~88% of system-prompt input cost on multi-turn sessions; (c) effort: 10 min check, possibly zero code change if pi already does this. *Highest ROI by far — dominates everything below.*\n2. **Move the `Current date:` line to a user-turn prefix instead of the system block.** (a) one line; (b) saves one cache-rewrite per day per session (~$0.015/session); (c) effort: trivial, but only worth doing as part of (1).\n3. **Trim the skills listing to name + one-line description, drop the embedded `<location>` paths.** (a) ~25 skills × ~80 chars saved ≈ 2K chars / ~500 tokens; (b) effort: small, requires changing how pi assembles the skills block; (c) only worth doing if you observe quality regressions traceable to the skills text — Anthropic explicitly warns against \"stuffing a laundry list\" [https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents].\n4. **Otherwise: leave it alone.** We're 2.5× smaller than Cursor, 3× smaller than Cline, and 4–6× smaller than loaded Claude Code. The cost of further trimming exceeds the benefit until measurable quality issues appear.\n\n## Caveats\n- The 4-chars-per-token heuristic understates token count for code/markdown by ~10–20%; real count via Anthropic's token-counting endpoint would be ~3,900–4,300 tk. Doesn't change the verdict.\n- No public data found for Continue or Cody — treated as unavailable.\n- \"Optimal length\" research (Particula's 4K/5.5K knee) is task-dependent; pi's task mix (tool-heavy coding agent) isn't a perfect match for the benchmarks behind those numbers.\n- We didn't measure pi's *actual* per-request token cost end-to-end (system + tools + history); the verdict assumes the rest of the request is well-behaved.\n\nThe prompt's fine. Now go forth and review some sessions — and if the prompt ever does get fat, at least we'll know exactly which skill to put on a diet first.",
